Third-party core
-----------------
This extension consumes repo-tour (https://github.com/BMA-Corgea/repo-tour), also licensed
AGPL-3.0-or-later, as its core engine: tree-sitter extraction, the digest pipeline, the LLM
layer, and the shared skins. repo-tour is loaded at build and run time from a local checkout
(the buildTutorials.repoTourPath setting) via dynamic import — it is a dependency, not code
copied into this repository, and this NOTICE does not modify repo-tour's own licensing.

Third-party icons
-----------------
The panels draw Visual Studio Code's own icons, the Codicons (https://github.com/microsoft/vscode-codicons),
Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation, from the npm package @vscode/codicons. Their font
(codicon.ttf) is shipped UNMODIFIED inside the extension at node_modules/@vscode/codicons/dist/,
and is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence (CC-BY-4.0,
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/; full text in node_modules/@vscode/codicons/LICENSE).
The few style rules that draw the icons (src/webview/codicons.ts) are adapted from the package's
codicon.css, which is licensed under the MIT licence (node_modules/@vscode/codicons/LICENSE-CODE).
Both are provided as is, without warranties of any kind; see those licences. Nothing here is
fetched from the network: the font is served to each panel from the extension's own install.

Your AGPL entitlement
-----------------------
The AGPL's network-use clause (section 13) means that if you run a modified version of this
extension where other people can interact with it over a network, they are entitled to that
modified version's source. Because a local VS Code extension is not, itself, network-facing
in that sense, this mainly matters if this extension's design changes to add a served
component later — see README.md's Source link, which is where that offer belongs either way.
